msgstr ""

#: ../../source/locations-attributes.rst:196
msgid "This value is a Universally Unique Identifier (UUID) generated using a computer program. Every location has a UUID to distinguish it from any other location. This field is used in data entry for :ref:`incident` in :ref:`incident:location:refs:assertion` and :ref:`positionings` in :ref:`positioning:location:refs:assertion`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/locations-attributes.rst:200
msgid "location:humane_id:qa"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/locations-attributes.rst:205
msgid "A human-readable unique identifier for each Location in the dataset."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/locations-attributes.rst:210 ../../source/locations-attributes.rst:249
msgid "Text string."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/locations-attributes.rst:225
msgid "``Ta'izz Governorate (osm, poly) 5c35b342-0b5e-4648-86cd-7ad730d647fa``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/locations-attributes.rst:230
msgid "Entries for the :ref:`location:humane_id:qa` field should combine information from several other location fields to give a researcher a quick, human readable way to understand a location. Best practice is to include the :ref:`name:annotation <location-name-annotation>`, :ref:`origin:location`, :ref:`geo_type:qa` and :ref:`id:entity` as this highlights the most important information while also creating a unique name due to the inclusion of :ref:`id:entity`. SFM adopts this approach with :ref:`location:humane_id:qa` with entries in the field following the below format:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/locations-attributes.rst:232
msgid "Example"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/locations-attributes.rst:234
msgid "The value ``Ta'izz Governorate (osm, poly) 5c35b342-0b5e-4648-86cd-7ad730d647fa``, which is a combination of multiple fields: ``name:annotation`` (``origin:location``, ``geo_type:qa``) ``id:entity``, tells us that the name of the place is ``Ta'izz Governorate``, that it is a Location found in ``osm`` (short for \"OpenStreetMap\") that it denotes an area (``poly``); the UUID provides the hard link to a specific attribute in the Location table."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/locations-attributes.rst:239
msgid "name:annotation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/locations-attributes.rst:244
msgid "The name of the Location as specified in the source of geospatial information from which it is taken."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/locations-attributes.rst:264
msgid "``Ta'izz Governorate``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/locations-attributes.rst:269
msgid "The value in :ref:`name:annotation <location-name-annotation>` is to be taken directly from the geospatial data source. For example, if a Location is derived from OpenStreetMap, we take the value from OSM's own \"name\" attribute and place it in :ref:`name:annotation <location-name-annotation>`. Along with :ref:`origin_id:location` and :ref:`geo_type:qa`, :ref:`name:annotation <location-name-annotation>` is needed for automation tools to identify the object within the geospatial data source. Where a Location is arbitrarily-defined, or is derived from a data source that does not provide a name, the Staff Researcher can provide one."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/locations-attributes.rst:273
msgid "origin:location"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/locations-attributes.rst:278
msgid "The geospatial information source that provides information about this Location."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/locations-attributes.rst:283 ../../source/locations-attributes.rst:317 ../../source/locations-attributes.rst:425 ../../source/locations-attributes.rst:459 ../../source/locations-attributes.rst:492 ../../source/locations-attributes.rst:528 ../../source/locations-attributes.rst:562 ../../source/locations-attributes.rst:596 ../../source/locations-attributes.rst:630 ../../source/locations-attributes.rst:664 ../../source/locations-attributes.rst:698 ../../source/locations-attributes.rst:732 ../../source/locations-attributes.rst:766 ../../source/locations-attributes.rst:800 ../../source/locations-attributes.rst:834 ../../source/locations-attributes.rst:868 ../../source/locations-attributes.rst:1106 ../../source/locations-attributes.rst:1140 ../../source/locations-attributes.rst:1176
msgid "String"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/locations-attributes.rst:298
msgid "``osm``, ``sfm``, ``hdx``, ``mimu``, ``khrg``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/locations-attributes.rst:303
msgid "The values in :ref:`origin:location` identify where automation tools should go to obtain spatial information about an object. For example, if the value ``osm`` is entered in :ref:`origin:location` this indicates that the automation tool should query OpenStreetMap in order to obtain spatial information about a Location. If ``osm`` were set, then the values in :ref:`name:annotation <location-name-annotation>` and :ref:`origin_id:location` would correspond to the object name and ID number in OpenStreetMap. Locations can be derived from comprehensive online services, as well as other sources like locally-held ``.shp`` or ``.kml`` files. The number of origins is unlimited."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/locations-attributes.rst:307
msgid "origin_id:location"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/locations-attributes.rst:312
msgid "The identifier for the Location as specified in the source of geospatial information from which it is taken."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/locations-attributes.rst:332
msgid "``383895``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/locations-attributes.rst:337
msgid "The value in :ref:`origin_id:location` is to be taken directly from the geospatial data source. For example, if a Location is derived from OpenStreetMap, we take the value from OSM's \"id\" attribute and place it in the :ref:`origin_id:location` attribute. Along with :ref:`name:annotation <location-name-annotation>` and :ref:`geo_type:qa`, :ref:`origin_id:location` is needed in order for automation tools to identify the object within the geospatial data source. Where a Location is arbitrarily defined, or is derived from a data source that does not provide an ID number, the Staff Researcher can provide one."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/locations-attributes.rst:341
msgid "geo_type:qa"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/locations-attributes.rst:346
msgid "The two-dimensional geometric primitive of the Location, as defined in the source of geospatial information from which it is taken."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/locations-attributes.rst:351
msgid "Text"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/locations-attributes.rst:366
msgid "``point``, ``poly``, ``line``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/locations-attributes.rst:371
msgid "This attribute uses a controlled vocabulary to describe the type of geometry used to represent the Location on a map. The Staff Researcher can choose from the following three options:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/locations-attributes.rst:373
msgid "``point``: the Location is a single distinct point on a map, represented by a single pair of geographic coordinates."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/locations-attributes.rst:374
msgid "``poly``: the Location is a closed area on a map, its boundary described by a sequence of geographic coordinates."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/locations-attributes.rst:375
msgid "``line``: the Location is a line on a map, described by a sequence of geographic coordinates. A ``line`` may also be closed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/locations-attributes.rst:377
msgid "The gazetteer used as the source of geometry may use different terminology to describe the Location. For example, in OpenStreetMap the boundaries of administrative areas (such as counties or states) `are described <https://wiki.openstreetmap.org/wiki/Relation>`_ using an object called a \"relation\"; although this can be a complex mix of different objects, for our purposes it is a ``poly`` because it describes an area."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/locations-attributes.rst:381
msgid "country:annotation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/locations-attributes.rst:386
msgid "Country in which the Location is situated."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/locations-attributes.rst:391
msgid "Text, controlled vocabulary"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/locations-attributes.rst:406
msgid "``ye``, ``ng``, ``mm``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/locations-attributes.rst:411
msgid "Values for this attribute are the ISO 3166-1 alpha-2 country codes, which can be found `on the ISO website <https://www.iso.org/obp/ui/#search/code/>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/locations-attributes.rst:415
msgid "geometry:ref:entity"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/locations-attributes.rst:420
msgid "The unique id generated by importer for the geojson for this Location."
msgstr ""

